tion, beats everything.\n        13. Quadplex set: a quad with two single cards OR two pairs attached.\n        Use the EXACT card symbols provided in the Valid Moves list.\n        Prepare a valid JSON object like {"cards": ["♠3", "♥3"]} for your answer, along with your explanation and reason. If you pass, output: {"cards": []}\n        \n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output the action with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n    ', 'user_prompt': "You are playing as a peasant. Your aim is to be the play out all your cards before the landlord does, OR help your teammate (the other peasant) play out their cards before the landlord does. If the landlord plays out all cards first, you lose.\nYou are a Peasant. The Previous Player (Left) is the Landlord (ENEMY). The Next Player (Right) is your TEAMMATE (Peasant). You must cooperate with your teammate to beat the Landlord. The objective is to empty your hand before the landlord does, or help your teammate empty their hand before the landlord does.\n\nYour Hand: ♠8 ♥9 ♣9 ♦10 ♠J ♦J ♥A ♣A ♣2 ♦2\nLast Play to Beat: ♥8\n--- Game History (Recent) ---\nTurn 1 (You): Pass\nTurn 2 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 2 (Prev Player): ♦6\nTurn 2 (You): ♠Q\nTurn 3 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 3 (Prev Player): ♠2\nTurn 3 (You): BlackJoker\nTurn 4 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 4 (Prev Player): Pass\nTurn 4 (You): ♣4 ♦5 ♥6 ♠7 ♦8\nTurn 5 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 5 (Prev Player): ♣10 ♣J ♣Q ♠K ♠A\nTurn 5 (You): Pass\nTurn 6 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 6 (Prev Player): ♥8\n--- Player Info ---\nCards Held by Next Player (Right) [Peasant (Teammate)]: 17\nCards Held by Prev Player (Left) [Landlord (Opponent)]: 6\n--- Cards Remaining Unknown (Card Counting) ---\n(Format explanation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nRedJoker: 1, 2: 1, A: 1, K: 3, Q: 2, J: 1, 10: 2, 9: 2, 8: 1, 7: 3, 6: 2, 5: 1, 4: 1, 3: 2\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♥9\nsingle: ♦10\nsingle: ♠J\nsingle: ♥A\nsingle: ♦2\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '**Analyzing the Board and Strategizing the Play**\n\nOkay, so here\'s the situation: I\'m the Peasant, and the Landlord just played a single ♥8. My hand is actually pretty strong. I\'ve got a lot of pairs: 9s, Jacks, Aces, and even a couple of 2s. The Landlord has only six cards left, which is dangerous, and my teammate has a whopping seventeen cards! I need to do something here, and quickly, to keep control.\n\nThe Landlord’s single 8 is a medium-small card. I know that if I just play a slightly higher single, like a 9 or 10, the Landlord might just let it go. They will be saving a high card, so they’ll pass it to my teammate with 17 cards! I don’t want to hand the initiative to the Landlord, and I certainly don\'t want to get into a battle with my teammate. I am the "guard," the "lower house," and it’s my job to prevent the Landlord from shedding low cards. However, I need to know what to play to keep control.\n\nMy hand is built for pairs. My 99, JJ, AA, and 22 are potential heavy hitters. My 22 in particular is a key asset since there\'s one 2, and the Red Joker is out there. No bombs, according to my count, which is excellent. I\'m keeping my eye on that Red Joker. It can throw off any strategy, and it’s likely that the Landlord has it, but based on the limited information that I have, they might not! So, the question is how to use my strength and to make sure I’m not playing into their hands.\n\nI\'m thinking, if I can lead with my pair of Aces, I\'m almost certainly in a good spot. So, here’s my plan: the Landlord has a single 8. Let’s shut that down! I\'m going to play my ♦2. This forces a response, since it’s higher than the 8. The Landlord either has to play their Red Joker (and get rid of the joker, which is good for me) or pass, giving me the lead. 22 is the highest pair, and even if they have some crazy high cards, I have a pair of Aces. I can handle it. I might even be able to play my pairs!\n\nI have no need to keep a pair of 2s because I have the Ace in my hand! Then the plan is simple: I lead with my 9s, then my Jacks, and then my Aces. I have two singles, 8 and 10, that I can use to finish. I\'ll take it from there. I will win, and I\'m going to help my teammate win too. This is the move.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord has 6 cards remaining, which is a dangerous state. They played a single ♥8. I have a very strong hand with multiple high pairs (AA, 22) and the card counts indicate that no player can have a bomb (maximum unknown count for any rank is 3). Since one Ace and one 2 are already played or unknown (I hold two of each), my pair of Aces is effectively the highest pair possible in the game (the "Nut Pair"), as no one can have AA or 22. Therefore, I can afford to break my pair of 2s to play a single 2. This move guarantees I either win the initiative or force the Landlord to play the Red Joker (the only card that can beat a 2, given no bombs). Gaining the lead is crucial to run my pairs (99, JJ, AA) and control the game.\n\nReason: Playing ♦2 is the most aggressive and safe move to stop the Landlord. It forces the Red Joker or wins the trick. Breaking the 22 pair is acceptable because my AA pair is invincible (no bombs or higher pairs exist). This sets up a winning sequence where I can lead my pairs.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦2"]}'}
